      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Your results tell a pretty clear story:\n",
        "\n",
        "The model is very good on A (Walking) and B (Jogging) and decent on C (Stairs).\n",
        "\n",
        "But many classes (D, G, H, J, etc.) have near-zero recall → the model is basically not learning them at all.\n",
        "\n",
        "That almost always happens because of how the windows are being created (most common), and/or because phone accelerometer alone doesn’t contain enough information to separate many “fine-grained” actions (eating pasta vs drinking vs folding clothes) reliably.\n",
        "\n",
        "The fastest, most effective fix you should do next:\n",
        "\n",
        "Fix the windowing: don’t window across mixed activities\n",
        "\n",
        "Right now you’re likely windowing across the entire subject stream after sorting by timestamp, which can mix different activities inside a window (or create noisy windows). Even if you use “purity”, it still causes lots of dropped/noisy windows and weak learning.\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead:\n",
        "✅ create windows within each (subject, activity) separately\n",
        "✅ also split into continuous segments if there are timestamp jumps\n",
        "\n",
        "Below is runnable code (not just functions) that:\n",
        "\n",
        "rebuilds windows correctly (subject+activity)\n",
        "\n",
        "trains a small power-friendly CNN\n",
        "\n",
        "uses class weights\n",
        "\n",
        "evaluates again"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ur9yexUm_QbU"
      }
